// Lesson 7.2 — Tile Collision, Layers & Sorting
// The Platformer scene's `Collision` tilemap ALREADY carries TilemapCollider2D
// (Composite Operation: Merge), CompositeCollider2D and a Static Rigidbody2D.
// Tilemap collision already works. This lesson is about USING and TUNING it:
// the physics layers, the 6.2 ground-check mask, sorting, and one-way platforms.
//
// Scaffolding: OUTLINE ONLY (level 3), and a BUILD lesson — there is no new
// script to write, so it keeps the code chip on the ask banner (do NOT pass the
// `concept` frame). The ask banner is the level marker: a level-3 lesson must
// not render a walkthrough, and verify-site refuses one that does (the
// `level >= 3 && wt` rule in scripts/verify-site.mjs).
//
// WHY THIS IS THE RUNG: the ladder is per concept, not per unit. 7.1 was the
// first treatment of painting; 7.2 is the same tileset again, asked to carry
// physics. The genuinely new idea is not a mechanism but a *classification* —
// "solid" and "decorative" are two jobs that one tilemap cannot hold, and
// "physics layer" and "sorting layer" are two lists with one word in common.
// Deciding which bucket a thing belongs in is a decision, which is what level 3
// is for.
//
// STRUCTURE (this pass): the discovery, the layer work and the collision matrix
// are ONE numbered, pane-named "In the Editor" list; the specifications are
// "In the Code", which is where a lesson with no new file keeps its
// specification and its Socratic. The old "Read Function-Level Examples" block
// is CUT WHOLE — airport security, hospital triage, a restaurant pass, a
// brewery valve and air-traffic altitude bands. Five unanchored snippets, none
// from a game, all of them illustrating a filter the student is about to tick
// by hand in a dropdown; the specification callout that lived in that block is
// anchored in the 6.2 mask the student actually owns, and it MOVED to "In the
// Code" rather than being cut with the block around it.
//
// CULLED, with the reason each was a repeat:
//   - Six lines of the "Already done for you" callout that restated the table
//     directly above it (Composite Operation = Merge, Body Type = Static). The
//     table is the container the student fills by reading the Inspector, so the
//     table survives and the restatement goes. What is left of the callout is
//     the one thing the table does NOT say: do not add a second tilemap
//     collider.
//   - The "Layer or tag" trap was three paragraphs of prose around one fact. The
//     fact — an empty mask is not a wide mask — is kept in full, with the
//     evidence that makes it real (the GetMask error, the Nothing dropdown, the
//     player who jumps in mid-air forever); the restatement of tag-versus-layer
//     in three different phrasings collapsed to one closing sentence.
//   - Two of the six sorting bullets: "which list answers who is allowed to
//     collide" (already the closing beat of the specification above it, and
//     already the lesson-lead's premise) and "read them as they are" folded
//     into the bullet that names the placeholder layers.
//   - The "is the mask set to Default or Everything" half of the ground-check
//     bullet, which asked the student to imagine a wrong value they were about
//     to see for themselves in step 5 and then again in the bullet below it.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read out of AP_CS_P_Starter_Project, Unity 6000.6.0f1):
//   - Assets/_Scenes/{Start,Overworld,Platformer}.unity; EditorBuildSettings
//     gives Start 0, Overworld 1, Platformer 2.
//   - Platformer has exactly three tilemaps. `Collision` (tag `Ground`) and
//     `Cave Exit` (Untagged) EACH carry TilemapCollider2D
//     (m_CompositeOperation: 1 = Merge, m_UsedByEffector: 0), CompositeCollider2D
//     and a Rigidbody2D with m_BodyType: 2 = STATIC, m_GravityScale: 1.
//     The third, `No Collision`, has a TilemapRenderer and NO collider of any
//     kind — pure paint, so no raycast can ever hit it. (An earlier lesson
//     wrongly paired `Collision` with `No Collision`; that is not repeated.)
//   - THE LAYER TRAP, and the reason this file was rewritten. TagManager.asset
//     defines the tags Coin, Enemy, Axe, Spikes, Ground, GroundCheck — and the
//     project shipped with NO `Ground` LAYER at all (the first empty user layer
//     is index 8; the only named user layers are the placeholders "Layer 1/2/3"
//     at indices 20-22). 6.2 is the lesson where the student CREATES the Ground
//     layer and puts `Collision` and `Cave Exit` in it, so by 7.2 it exists —
//     but the failure mode of a mask naming a layer that does not exist is
//     silent and is NOT what this page used to say. `LayerMask.GetMask("Ground")`
//     logs an error and returns an EMPTY mask — it does not widen to everything
//     — so the ground check reads false on a floor you can see and the player
//     jumps in mid-air forever. An unset Inspector LayerMask is `Nothing`, which
//     is the same silence by another route. The old text claimed the mask
//     "defaulted to everything"; that was wrong and is fixed below.
//   - CORRECTED IN AN EARLIER REWRITE: the old Step 1 said "There is no Ground
//     layer in this project" while the old Step 2 said "You already created
//     Ground back in 6.2" — a flat self-contradiction. Both halves are true: the
//     project SHIPPED without it, and 6.2 is where it gets made.
//   - Also corrected: the ground-check callout used to say one of the objects
//     answering a Default-mask raycast in the Platformer scene "can shoot".
//     Nothing in that scene shoots — it contains one prefab instance (the
//     Player), the three tilemaps, Main Camera, a Quad backdrop (a 3D
//     MeshCollider, invisible to Physics2D) and `Cave Exit`. Turrets live in
//     the Overworld.
//   - Sorting layers are literally named Default, Bottom, Layer 2, Player,
//     Layer 4, Top. `Layer 2`/`Layer 4` are an unrenamed wart pending an editor
//     rename; this page teaches them as they are and asks the student what a
//     placeholder name costs.
//   - Physics2DSettings.asset: m_QueriesStartInColliders: 1, m_QueriesHitTriggers: 1,
//     m_Gravity: {x: 0, y: -9.81}.
//   - Player.prefab and Turret.prefab carry NO Collider2D and NO Rigidbody2D.
//     Turret.prefab and Turret_Projectile.prefab are BOTH tagged `Enemy`.
//     The scripts in Assets/Scripts are stubs, so a value set in the Inspector
//     lives on the prefab instance in the scene, never "in the script".
//
// PROTECTED BLOCKS — ids are POSITIONAL (see src/lib/formify.ts), so nothing
// here is reordered, renumbered or re-counted. The inventory as it stands, in
// document order:
//   socratic  "Reading a Component You Did Not Write"  3 items  (3 boxed)
//   socratic  "Choose, Then Justify"                   3 items  (3 boxed)
//   checklist                                          9 items (NOT
//                                          harvested; the two ladder lines
//                                          match 4.2 and 6.4)
// Both socratics keep their slots, their order and their wording; the blocks
// around them moved, which is not a question-block edit. The bug-hunt and
// tinker blocks that used to sit after the socratics were removed by an earlier
// block cut, so no 7.2:bh1:* or 7.2:tk1:* id is produced any more.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';
import type { DecomposeSpec } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The recurring two-beat block: split the problem, look each part up, connect
// it to something already built. Level 3, so the renderer withholds what each
// part DOES and asks the student to say it — the parts and their doc pointers
// are the level-3 bargain. Every part carries a `doc` (verify-site: docs ===
// parts at level <= 3) and every doc pointer is either a place in the project
// they can open today or a lesson they have finished (1.3, 2.1, 6.2, 7.1) —
// nothing later than today. No `blank` (that is the level-2 mechanism), and no
// third `concept` argument on renderDecompose: this is a build lesson, so it
// keeps the code chip.
const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big:
    'The cave floor already holds you up and the player already draws in front of the backdrop, ' +
    'and you wrote neither of those facts. So the job is not "make a tilemap solid" — it is to ' +
    'find out which components somebody else left on the tilemap, give the project the physics ' +
    'layers it shipped without, and separate what is solid from what is only painted. As one ' +
    'task it is unstartable, because most of the answer is already sitting in an Inspector and ' +
    'none of it is in a script.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Read the three components you inherited',
      does:
        'A Tilemap Collider 2D that builds one small shape per painted tile, a Composite Collider 2D that fuses those shapes into a single outline, and the Static Rigidbody 2D that lets the merged outline be a body the physics engine owns. You added none of them, and the floor does not work unless all three are there.',
      doc: 'the component table in the first activity on this page, and the Inspector reading you did on this same tilemap in 6.2',
      href: '6.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Prove the composite is really merging',
      does:
        'The tilemap collider\'s Composite Operation setting is what feeds the composite. Put it back to None and the floor still holds you up while the seams between tiles come back — the merge is the thing being checked here, not the solidity.',
      doc: 'the Composite Operation row in the tilemap collider\'s Inspector, and the merge question in the first Socratic on this page',
    },
    {
      name: 'Create the layers the project shipped without',
      does:
        'Rename empty slots in the Layers list. A layer is project-wide rather than per-scene, so this is done once for the whole game — and a name typed here is the only thing a mask can ever be pointed at.',
      doc: 'the Layers list in Edit → Project Settings → Tags and Layers, and the layer you created there in 6.2',
      href: '6.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Assign each layer on the prefab, not the instance',
      does:
        'A layer set on one instance is a fact about that object in that scene; a layer set on the prefab asset is inherited by every copy of it, everywhere, without anybody remembering to do it again.',
      doc: 'the prefab-versus-instance rule from 2.1',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Point the ground mask at a layer that exists',
      does:
        'The LayerMask field from 6.2 is a filter, and a filter needs something real to filter on. A mask naming a layer nobody made does not widen to everything — it goes empty, and an empty mask means the ray is allowed to notice nothing at all.',
      doc: '6.2 — Ground Checks: No Infinite Jumps, where the mask field and the ground check live',
      href: '6.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide the collision matrix, pair by pair',
      does:
        'The grid of tick-boxes in Physics 2D is the second half of the layer system: the mask says what a query may notice, the matrix says what is allowed to touch what at all. Turning one pair off is how a projectile stops hitting the turret that fired it.',
      doc: 'the layer collision matrix in Edit → Project Settings → Physics 2D',
    },
    {
      name: 'Separate the solid from the painted',
      does:
        'Paint is not physics until a collider says it is, and this cave already ships one tilemap that is pure paint. Either paint decoration onto that one, or keep it on a solid tilemap and give that tilemap a layer your queries exclude — and then defend the choice.',
      doc: 'the trap callouts in the In the Editor half of this page, and the three tilemaps in the Platformer scene',
      href: '7.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Sort what draws in front of what',
      does:
        'Sorting layers are a second, unrelated list that happens to share the word. They decide draw order and nothing else: no sorting layer can stop the player falling through a floor, and no physics layer can put a pillar in front of them.',
      doc: 'the Sorting Layers list in Edit → Project Settings → Tags and Layers',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Seven of those eight parts are Inspector work and not one of them is code — which is why the ' +
    'split has to be written down. A task you cannot see as a list of places to go and look is a ' +
    'task you will try to solve by writing a script, and there is no script that can help you ' +
    'here. Look at the shape: the colliders were already there, the layers are names you type ' +
    'once, one of the two lists called "layer" has nothing to do with physics, and the last two ' +
    'parts are decisions about categories rather than about objects.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: does the part name something you could open and point at — a ' +
    'component, a list, a checkbox, a prefab? "Make the tilemap solid" points at nothing, and it ' +
    'is also already done. "Which setting feeds the composite" points at one checkbox, and that ' +
    'is a part.',
  turns: [
    'You are adding four layers, and every one is an empty bucket until something is put in it. Name one object in this project that sits on `Default` today and should not, and say what goes wrong the day a mask is pointed at `Default` instead of at a category you chose.',
    'Two sorting layers in this project are placeholders nobody replaced. Fix them before you build on top of them, and say what a placeholder name costs you three weeks from now.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>7.2 — Tile Collision, Layers &amp; Sorting</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your cave is already solid, you did not make it solid, and until today you could not have
    explained <em>why</em>. Three components work quietly under your feet, and the list of layers
    your own code from <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> gambles on was never finished. Today you find
    out what you inherited, and finish what you started.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('why is my tilemap solid, and why is my player in front of it?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('7.2', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~40 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Press Play and stand on the floor, then <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select
        <code>Collision</code> and read <em>every</em> component on it in the
        <strong>Inspector</strong>. Three of them you did not add, and each is load-bearing:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Component</th><th>What it contributes</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Tilemap Collider 2D</code></td>
            <td>Turns tiles into physics shapes: <strong>one small collider per solid tile</strong>, so the cave becomes a few hundred shapes.</td>
            <td>Every shape is a candidate in every physics query. It works, and it is quietly expensive.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Composite Collider 2D</code></td>
            <td>Fuses those shapes into <strong>one merged outline</strong>: the tilemap collider feeds it, and it publishes a single clean polygon.</td>
            <td>One more component to understand, and one setting that decides whether it is used at all.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody 2D</code></td>
            <td>Unity adds this with the composite. It lets the merged shape be a body the physics engine owns.</td>
            <td>Nothing, if you leave it alone — but it must stay <strong>Static</strong>. A floor that moves is a bug in a cave.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        So the work is not <em>adding</em> collision. Work the list in order — every step names
        its pane.
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → the Tilemap Collider 2D →</strong> the
            <strong>Composite Operation</strong> dropdown. It reads <strong>Merge</strong>, and
            that setting decides whether the composite above it is used at all (older docs call
            the checkbox <em>Used By Composite</em>). Set it to <strong>None</strong>, press Play
            and walk: you still stand up, and the seams come back as lips you catch on. Set it
            back to <strong>Merge</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Edit ▸ Project Settings ▸ Tags and Layers →</strong> compare the two lists.
            <strong>Tags</strong> holds six names, two of them <code>Ground</code> and
            <code>GroundCheck</code>. <strong>Layers</strong> is where the gap shows: as the
            project <em>shipped</em> it held the Unity defaults, a run of empty slots and three
            placeholders called <code>Layer 1</code>, <code>Layer 2</code> and
            <code>Layer 3</code> — and <strong>no <code>Ground</code> layer at all</strong>.
            <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> is where you typed one into the first empty slot; confirm
            it is still there, because every mask in this unit points at it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Same window →</strong> finish the list. <code>Ground</code> is one layer, and
            one bucket is not a layer system. Add <code>Player</code>, <code>Enemy</code>,
            <code>Projectile</code> and <code>Decoration</code>, renaming empty slots as you go.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window → <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> → Inspector on each prefab →</strong>
            assign them at the source. The two solid tilemaps become <code>Ground</code>, the
            Player prefab <code>Player</code>, the enemy and turret prefabs <code>Enemy</code>,
            the projectile prefab <code>Projectile</code>, and anything you paint that is only
            scenery <code>Decoration</code>. A layer set on one instance is a fact about that
            object; <strong>on the prefab</strong>, every copy inherits it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → the Player → Inspector →</strong> the 6.2 ground-check component.
            Point its mask field at <code>Ground</code> and nothing else, then test the claim:
            stand next to an enemy and ask the ground check whether you are grounded.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Edit ▸ Project Settings ▸ Physics 2D →</strong> the layer collision matrix,
            the grid of tick-boxes. Decide, pair by pair, who collides with whom, then justify two
            ticks: <code>Enemy</code> against <code>Enemy</code>?
            <code>Projectile</code> against <code>Decoration</code>?
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Already done for you — do not do it twice.</strong> Follow an older tutorial that
        adds a <strong>second</strong> Tilemap Collider 2D to <code>Collision</code> and you do
        not get twice the colliders: you get duplicated shapes and edge behaviour you will blame
        on your movement code. Read the Inspector before you add any component in this unit.
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Layer or tag — they are not the same tool, and this project shipped with only
        one of them.</strong> The <code>Collision</code> tilemap carries the <em>tag</em>
        <code>Ground</code>, and that tag is what your <code>CompareTag()</code> checks read. But a
        raycast mask cannot use a tag — it needs a <em>layer</em>, and the project you were
        handed had none. The part that catches people: a mask naming a layer that does not
        exist does <strong>not</strong> quietly widen to everything.
        <code>LayerMask.GetMask("Ground")</code> in a project with no <code>Ground</code> layer
        logs an error and hands back a mask with <em>no bits set</em> — and an empty mask is not
        a wide mask. The ray notices nothing, the ground check reads false on a floor you can
        see, and the player jumps in mid-air forever. An unset Inspector dropdown is the same
        silence by another route: Unity draws it as <strong>Nothing</strong>. Prove it once, on
        purpose, by renaming the layer and watching the check stop working. Tags
        <em>identify</em> one object; layers <em>filter</em> whole categories in a query.
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Painting decoration onto the solid tilemap is the trap this whole lesson exists to close.</strong>
        A pebble, a vine, a distant rock — all of them look correct painted on
        <code>Collision</code>, and all of them become rock. Then the thing standing on the
        pebble is you. Two fixes are defensible; choose one and defend it: paint
        decoration onto the <code>No Collision</code> tilemap (simple, and it can never lie to
        you), or keep it on a solid tilemap and give that tilemap a <code>Decoration</code>
        layer your queries exclude (more flexible, more to remember).
    </div>
</div>

