<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        No new file today, so this half holds the two specifications. Write your answers down.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the ground check you already own.</strong> In
        <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> you wrote a <code>LayerMask</code> field and passed it into
        <code>Physics2D.Raycast</code>. Open that script and answer <em>before you touch
        anything</em>:
        <ul>
            <li>What is that mask field currently set to in the Inspector? Not what you intended — what is actually ticked in the dropdown.</li>
            <li>Name the two solid surfaces in this cave that a <code>Ground</code> mask includes and a <code>Default</code> mask does not — and say which of the two you would have forgotten to put in the bucket.</li>
            <li>The tag <code>Ground</code> already exists and is already on the <code>Collision</code> tilemap. If you could fix this with a tag instead of a layer, what would the raycast call look like, and what would it cost you every frame?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: sorting, the other kind of layer.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Read the <strong>Sorting Layers</strong> list. It contains <code>Bottom</code>, <code>Player</code> and <code>Top</code> — and also <code>Layer 2</code> and <code>Layer 4</code>, placeholders nobody ever replaced. Read them as they are, then say which of the two lists answers "who draws in front".</li>
            <li>Your backdrop, your ground, your player and your attack swing all need an order. Assign a sorting layer to each, and say why the swing effect needs its own layer above <code>Player</code>. Two sprites on the <em>same</em> layer still need an order: name that second knob and where it lives.</li>
            <li>Is a sorting layer a physics object in any way — can changing one stop the player falling through the floor? Prove it from the two lists.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Reading a Component You Did Not Write</h3>
        <ol>
<li>The tilemap collider builds a shape per tile, and the composite merges them. With the merge switched off, how many physics shapes would the cave have?</li>
<li>The rigidbody is Static. What does Static mean about the body's willingness to move, and why is that the correct choice for a cave floor rather than an oversight?</li>
<li>Standing on this floor, your ground check from <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> succeeds. So does the raycast care whether it hit one merged polygon or four hundred separate tile shapes?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: One-Way Platforms</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        A ledge you can jump <em>up through</em> is not a layer's job — it is a collider property
        with a <strong>direction</strong> (the effector's <strong>Surface Arc</strong> decides
        which way is up). Three honest ways to build one:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>What it costs</th><th>When it wins</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A separate one-way tilemap</strong> — its own Tilemap Collider 2D + Composite Collider 2D + <code>Platform Effector 2D</code>, with <em>Used By Effector</em> ticked</td>
            <td>The tilemap becomes a membrane you pass upward and stand on downward.</td>
            <td>A third tilemap to maintain, and one direction for every tile on it.</td>
            <td>Many one-way ledges, all behaving the same.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Per-tile effector</strong> — <code>Platform Effector 2D</code> plus <em>Used By Effector</em> on the tilemap collider</td>
            <td>Individual tiles in the existing tilemap gain a direction.</td>
            <td>It fights the composite merge, and you get two kinds of tile on one grid.</td>
            <td>Rarely. Know it exists; do not reach for it first.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A loose object</strong> — a GameObject with a Box Collider 2D + <code>Platform Effector 2D</code>, placed over a gap in the tiles</td>
            <td>One platform, one object, zero tilemap surgery.</td>
            <td>It is no longer part of the painted level, so it can drift out of alignment with the grid.</td>
            <td>You need exactly one or two, and you need them now.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Choose, Then Justify</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Which of the three approaches did you build, and what in your level made it the right one?</li>
<li>A one-way platform that is one tile thick: what happens to the player who is <em>inside</em> it when the effector is added mid-game? Predict before testing.</li>
<li>If a one-way ledge sits on a layer excluded from the player's mask, does the player still stand on it? Which system decides that — the layer mask or the effector?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>


